        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A chainsaw chain should be snug against the guide bar, with no sag along the underside, yet still loose enough that you can pull it around the bar by hand with a gloved hand and moderate effort. If you pull the chain away from the middle of the bar, the drive links should not come fully out of the bar groove, and when you let go it should snap straight back. Too loose and the chain can jump off the bar; too tight and it overheats and wears out the bar, the sprocket and the chain itself. Getting it right takes a minute, but it has to be done with the bar nuts loosened, the bar nose held up, and an eye on whether the chain is hot or cold.
        </p>

        <div id="right-tension" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Does Correct Chain Tension Look Like?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Correct tension is a balance between two failure points. The chain has to be tight enough that it stays seated in the bar groove at full speed and under load, and loose enough that it runs freely around the bar and the nose sprocket without dragging. Chainsaw makers describe the target in nearly the same words: the chain sits snugly against the underside of the bar, and it can still be pulled along the bar by hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the underside of the bar first. On a correctly tensioned chain, the tie straps along the bottom sit right against the bar rails with no visible gap or droop. If you can see the chain hanging down in the middle of the bar, even slightly, it is too loose. Then try to move the chain along the top of the bar with a gloved hand. It should slide with steady, moderate effort, not glide freely and not refuse to budge.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pull test confirms it. Grab a cutter near the middle of the top of the bar and pull it straight up, away from the bar. The chain should lift a little, but the drive links, the small fins that ride in the bar groove, should not come all the way out. Let go and the chain should snap back against the bar. That snap is the sign of a chain under the right tension; a chain that stays lifted or sags back slowly needs tightening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Different saws do not change the target. A small electric pruning saw, a 20-inch gas saw and a pole saw all want the same snug-but-pullable fit, though the force you feel varies with chain size. If you are still choosing a saw, our guide to the <a href="/best-chainsaw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best chainsaws</a> notes which models make tensioning easy and which bury the adjuster behind the clutch cover.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No sag underneath, pullable by hand, snaps back.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The chain should sit against the underside of the bar with no droop, move around the bar by gloved hand with moderate effort, and snap back when pulled away. Drive links should never leave the groove completely.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="check-tension" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Do You Check Chainsaw Chain Tension?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Make the saw safe first. On a gas saw, the engine must be off and the spark plug lead pulled if you are doing more than a quick look. On a battery saw, remove the battery. On a corded saw, unplug it. Wear gloves, because a sharp chain cuts skin with almost no pressure. And release the chain brake before you try to move the chain, since an engaged brake locks the chain and makes every chain feel overtight.
          </p>

          <p>
            With the saw on a stable surface, look along the underside of the bar for sag, then move the chain around the bar by hand for one complete loop. You are feeling for consistency. A chain that is smooth for most of the loop but binds in one spot may have a stiff link, a burr on the bar rail or a damaged drive link, and tightening around that tight spot leaves the rest of the chain overtightened.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, do the pull test at the middle of the bar on the top side, where the chain is easiest to reach. Lift a cutter gently. If the drive link tangs come completely out of the groove, the chain is too loose. If you cannot lift it at all and the chain will barely move by hand, it is too tight. Somewhere between, with the tangs still partly in the groove and a firm snap back on release, is the right place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check tension before every use and again whenever you stop to refuel or swap batteries. Chains loosen as they warm up and as they wear in, so a chain that was perfect at the start of a job can be sagging an hour later. A quick look at the underside of the bar during each break takes seconds and catches the problem before the chain jumps off the bar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="adjust-tension" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do You Adjust Chainsaw Chain Tension?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a saw with a conventional screw tensioner, the bar is clamped by one or two bar nuts on the side cover, and a small tensioning screw, usually on the side of the bar or at the front of the cover, moves the bar forward or back. The adjustment only works with the bar nuts loosened, because the bar has to be free to slide. Trying to turn the tension screw against tight bar nuts strips the adjuster or bends the pin that engages the bar.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Step</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to Do</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why It Matters</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Engine off or battery out, gloves on, chain brake released</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Safety, and a free-moving chain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Loosen the bar nuts until they are just finger-tight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lets the bar slide without wobbling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lift the bar nose up and hold it there</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seats the bar in its working position</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Turn the tension screw until the chain sits snug under the bar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sets tension without overtightening</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keep the nose up and tighten the bar nuts fully</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Locks the bar where you set it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">6</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pull the chain around the bar and repeat the pull test</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Confirms the final tension</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The nose-up step is the one most people skip, and it is why their chain goes slack within a few cuts. When you cut, the chain pulls the bar nose upward. If you tighten the bar nuts with the nose resting low, the bar shifts up the first time it takes load, and the chain loosens. Holding the nose up while you set tension and while you tighten the nuts puts the bar where cutting forces will push it anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn the adjuster in small increments, a quarter turn or so at a time, and check after each one. On most saws turning the screw clockwise tightens the chain, but check your manual, since the direction varies with the adjuster's position. Stop as soon as the sag under the bar disappears. Going further than that point is where overtightening starts, and the difference between right and too tight is often less than a full turn.
          </p>

          <p>
            After tightening the bar nuts, run the full check again: no sag underneath, chain pulls around by hand, drive links stay in the groove on the pull test. If the chain is new, run the saw for a minute or two at moderate speed with plenty of bar oil, shut it off, and check again. New chains seat and stretch quickly at first, and they nearly always need another adjustment within the first few cuts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hot-vs-cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Should You Tighten a Chainsaw Chain Hot or Cold?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A chain expands as it heats from friction on the bar, which is why it gets visibly looser after several minutes of hard cutting. That slack is not a malfunction. Adjusting it while you work is fine, and often necessary, but a hot chain should not be tightened to the same snugness you would set on a cold one. When it cools, it contracts, and a chain set tight while hot can become overtight as it cools down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Overtightening by cooling does real damage. A contracting chain squeezes the bar, the nose sprocket and the drive sprocket, and on gas saws it puts load on the crankshaft bearings. In cold weather it can tighten enough to be hard to turn by hand. Manufacturers' advice is consistent: if you tension the chain while it is warm, loosen it again at the end of the work session before the saw cools.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, when you adjust a hot chain, take up only the obvious sag. The chain should still sit near the underside of the bar but feel a little looser than your cold setting, with the drive links staying in the groove on the pull test. Then, when you put the saw away, back the tension off slightly. The next time you start, check the cold tension and set it properly before cutting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Excess heat is also a warning. If the chain loosens dramatically after just a few minutes, the bar may be running dry. Check that the oil tank is full, that oil is actually reaching the bar, which you can test by running the saw at speed near a light surface and watching for a line of oil spatter, and that the oil port in the bar is clear. A dull chain also generates extra heat because you have to push it through the wood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tool-less" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do Tool-Less Chain Tensioners Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many homeowner gas saws and most battery saws use a tool-less tensioner. Instead of bar nuts and a screw, there is a large knob on the side cover that clamps the bar, plus a thumbwheel or dial that moves it. The principle is identical: loosen the clamping knob, hold the nose up, turn the wheel until the chain is snug, then tighten the knob firmly with the nose still up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The convenience is real. You can adjust tension in the field without hunting for a scrench, and it is easier to make small corrections during a job. The tradeoff is clamping force. A hand-tightened knob does not hold as firmly as nuts torqued with a wrench, so if it is not fully tightened, the bar can creep under vibration and the chain loosens. Tighten the knob hard and check tension a little more often than you would on a conventional saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some systems need cleaning to keep working. Sawdust and oil pack into the tensioner mechanism and the slot in the bar, which can make the wheel stiff or let the adjustment slip. Clean under the side cover whenever you remove it, and make sure the bar is seated properly on the tensioner pin or gear during reassembly, since a bar that is not engaged will not move when you turn the wheel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery saws follow the same tension rules, with one practical difference: they run cooler than gas saws, so you see less hot-and-cold drift. Pole saws, whether battery, electric or gas, use the same snug-but-pullable target, though checking at height means you should set tension carefully on the ground first. Our guide to the <a href="/best-pole-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best pole saws</a> notes which ones have the easiest adjusters to reach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-loose-too-tight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Happens If the Chain Is Too Loose or Too Tight?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A loose chain is the more dangerous problem. As it sags, the drive links ride higher in the groove and can climb out entirely, throwing the chain off the bar. That usually happens at the nose or as the chain comes off the drive sprocket, and a thrown chain can strike the operator; the chain catcher under the bar exists for exactly this reason. A loose chain also slaps against the bar, wears the rails unevenly, makes rough, wandering cuts and can contribute to kickback because it is not cutting cleanly at the nose.
          </p>

          <p>
            A tight chain fails more slowly but more expensively. It drags on the bar, so the saw bogs or a battery saw draws more power and runs down faster. The bar overheats, sometimes smoking or turning blue near the nose. The nose sprocket bearings and drive sprocket wear faster, and on gas saws the extra load reaches the clutch and crankshaft bearings. A tight chain also wears its own drive links and rivets faster, which shortens its life.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Symptom</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely Too Loose</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely Too Tight</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chain droops under the bar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chain hard or impossible to pull by hand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chain jumps or throws off the bar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smoke or discoloration at the bar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, or poor oiling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Saw bogs under light cuts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sometimes, from rough cutting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rapid rail or sprocket wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Uneven rail wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Overall wear and heat</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Some symptoms come from other problems, so rule them out before cranking the adjuster. A saw that cuts at an angle or wanders usually has a dull or unevenly sharpened chain, or a bar with worn rails, not a tension issue. A chain that smokes despite correct tension is often starved of oil. Keeping the cutters sharp prevents a lot of these headaches; our guide to the <a href="/best-chainsaw-sharpener/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best chainsaw sharpeners</a> covers files, guides and bench grinders.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a chain does come off the bar, stop and inspect before putting it back. Check the drive links for bent or burred tangs, look for damage to the bar rails and nose, and check the drive sprocket. A thrown chain often damages a link or two, and a damaged chain will keep jumping no matter how carefully you tension it. Replace damaged chains rather than running them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stretch-and-wear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Why Does My Chainsaw Chain Keep Getting Loose?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some loosening is normal. New chains seat in quickly during their first uses as the rivets and links bed in, so expect several adjustments during the first hour or two of cutting. Heat loosens any chain during work. And the bar may be creeping if the nuts or tool-less knob were not tightened firmly with the nose held up. If the chain sags within minutes every time, those three causes cover most cases.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over the longer term, chains stretch. The metal itself does not lengthen much; what happens is wear at every rivet and on the bearing surfaces of the links, and those tiny amounts across a hundred or so links add up to a longer chain. Eventually the tensioner runs out of travel and cannot take up any more slack. At that point the chain has reached the end of its life, even if the cutters still have material left.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chain wear and sprocket wear go together. A worn chain wears a matching pattern into the drive sprocket, and putting a new chain on a badly worn sprocket wears the new chain fast and can make it run rough. A common guideline from saw makers is to replace the drive sprocket at least every second chain, and sooner if the teeth show deep grooves. Check the bar at the same time: flip it periodically so it wears evenly, clean the groove and oil holes, and file off any burrs on the rails.
          </p>

          <p>
            Removing a link to shorten a stretched chain is technically possible with a chain breaker and rivet spinner, but it is rarely worth it for homeowners, because the stretched chain no longer meshes well with the sprocket. A replacement chain matched to your bar's pitch, gauge and drive link count is inexpensive compared with a new bar and sprocket. If you cut firewood every fall, keep a spare chain on hand along with the fuel and bar oil, and our <a href="/best-log-splitter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best log splitter</a> picks cover the next step. The same autumn yard day is a good moment to check our batch guide on <a href="/when-to-fertilize-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to fertilize the lawn</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adjusting with the bar nuts tight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The tension screw moves the bar, and the bar cannot move while it is clamped. Turning the adjuster against tight nuts strips the screw or bends the pin that engages the bar. Always loosen the bar nuts or tool-less knob to finger-tight first, then adjust, then clamp it down again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the nose drop before clamping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you set tension with the bar nose resting low, cutting forces push the nose up within a few cuts and the chain goes slack. Hold the nose up while you turn the adjuster and keep holding it up while you tighten the bar nuts or knob fully.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening a hot chain to cold tension</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A chain expands when hot and contracts as it cools. Setting a hot chain as snug as a cold one means it can become overtight as it cools, straining the bar, sprockets and bearings. Take up only the obvious sag while hot, and loosen slightly at the end of the session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overtightening to stop the chain throwing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cranking the chain tight because it came off once just trades one problem for another. A chain that keeps jumping usually has a damaged drive link, worn bar rails, a worn sprocket or a bar that is creeping. Find that cause, then set normal snug tension.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing with the chain brake on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An engaged chain brake locks the chain, so it feels impossible to pull around the bar even when tension is fine. People then loosen a correctly set chain until it sags. Release the brake, with the engine off or battery out, before you check or adjust tension.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I check chainsaw chain tension?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check before every use and every time you stop to refuel or swap batteries. Check more often with a new chain, which stretches quickly during its first hour or two, and after any hard cutting that heats the chain. A glance at the underside of the bar for sag takes seconds and prevents most thrown chains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which way do you turn the screw to tighten a chainsaw chain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most saws, turning the tensioning screw clockwise tightens the chain, but the direction depends on where the adjuster sits and how the saw is built, so check your manual. Loosen the bar nuts first, hold the nose up, and turn in small increments. If the sag gets worse after a quarter turn, you are going the wrong way.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my chainsaw chain smoking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Smoke at the bar usually means friction and heat. The common causes are a chain tensioned too tight, a bar that is not getting enough oil, a dull chain that you are forcing through the wood, or the chain brake partly engaged. Stop, let it cool, check the oil level and flow, then confirm tension with the pull test.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a chainsaw chain be too tight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A chain that will not pull around the bar by hand drags on the bar rails, overheats, and wears the bar, nose sprocket, drive sprocket and chain faster. On gas saws, it also loads the clutch and crankshaft bearings. A tight chain can still look fine, so always confirm you can move it by hand with the brake off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my new chainsaw chain keep loosening?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                New chains seat in and stretch slightly during their first uses as rivets and links bed in. That is normal. Run a new chain briefly at moderate speed with plenty of bar oil, then recheck and retension, and expect to adjust it several times in the first hour or two of cutting. After that, it should settle down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know when a chainsaw chain is worn out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Replace a chain when the tensioner reaches the end of its travel and cannot remove the slack, when cutters have been filed back to the wear marks on the cutter, or when links are cracked, bent or loose. A chain that keeps jumping despite correct tension, or that cuts poorly right after sharpening, is also due.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I loosen my chainsaw chain for storage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you tensioned it while warm, yes: back it off slightly before the saw cools, so the contracting chain does not overtighten against the bar. For longer storage, many owners leave the chain just slightly slack and retension it before the next use, which is a good habit in cold weather especially.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

