        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A chainsaw chain is tight enough when it sits against the underside of the bar with no sag, still pulls around by gloved hand, and snaps back when you lift it, with the drive links staying in the groove. Set it with the bar nuts loosened and the nose held up, tighten the nuts with the nose still up, and check it again every time you refuel.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Remember that heat loosens a chain and cooling tightens it, so go easy when adjusting a hot chain and back it off at the end of the day. When the adjuster runs out of travel, replace the chain and check the sprocket. If your saw makes this harder than it should be, our <a href="/best-chainsaw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best chainsaw</a> picks include models with tensioners that are easy to use.
